Next Word Predictor
========================================================
author: Luis Ruiz
date: May 2026
autosize: true

The Problem
========================================================

Typing on a phone is slow and error-prone.

A text prediction model can suggest the next word before the user finishes typing, reducing effort and mistakes.

- **Data**: 3 English corpora from SwiftKey — blogs, news, Twitter
- Over 4 million lines, ~100 million words
- **Goal**: given a short phrase, predict the most likely next word

The Algorithm
========================================================

**N-gram model with Stupid Backoff**

1. Sample 3% of each corpus and clean the text
2. Build frequency tables of bigrams (2-word sequences) and trigrams (3-word sequences)
3. Given user input, match the last 2 words against the trigram table
4. If no match found, fall back to the last word in the bigram table
5. If still no match, return the most common words overall

Pruning: n-grams with frequency < 2 are discarded. Only the top 5 predictions per prefix are kept. This keeps the model small and fast.

The App
========================================================

**How to use it**

1. Open the app at the link below
2. Type any phrase in English in the text box
3. Click **Predict**
4. The top 3 suggested next words appear instantly

The model is pre-built and stored as a single file loaded at startup. All lookups use named R lists — O(1) access with no database needed.

**App**: https://luisruizc.shinyapps.io/shiny_app/

Why It Works
========================================================

- Simple is fast: no neural network, no GPU required
- The SwiftKey corpus covers natural everyday English well
- Backoff ensures a prediction is always returned, even for unknown words
- Responds in under 50ms for any input

**Limitations and next steps**

- Larger training sample would improve coverage
- Adding 4-grams would improve accuracy for longer phrases
- Kneser-Ney smoothing for better probability estimates
